# The formatted-float rounding rule (QUICKSTART, "Format specifiers"; 2026-10-26, Opus + Fable
# under Sean's standing rule): `${x:.Nf}` / `${x:.N}` / `${x:.Ne}` round the EXACT BINARY VALUE,
# ties to even -- Python's digits and the UCRT's printf. 2.675 is stored as 2.67499999..., so it
# prints 2.67; 0.125 and 2.5 are exact halves and go to the even digit. Until 2026-10-26 Zebra
# rounded the shortest decimal half away from zero (2.68, 0.13, 3, -3), which is Zig's own
# `{d:.2}`. Verified against Python's format() over 300 values x 10 specs (all equal; the
# previous compiler differed on 106 of the 300).
def main()
    var a = 2.675
    var b = 0.125
    var c = 2.5
    var d = -2.5
    var e = 1.45
    var h = 0.5
    var o = 1.5
    var z = 0.0 * -1.0
    print("float_round: ${a:.2f} ${b:.2f} ${c:.0f} ${d:.0f} ${e:.1f} ${a:.2} ${a:.2e}")
    print("float_round_halves: ${h:.0f} ${o:.0f} ${z:.2f} ${9.996:.2f} ${9.996:.2e}")
    print("float_round_pad: [${a:8.2f}] [${a:<8.2f}] [${a:*^9.2f}]")
